"""Module 2, Lesson 9: Linux Capstone: Restore the Service."""

LESSON = {'id': '2-9', 'title': 'Linux Capstone: Restore the Service', 'summary': 'Take ownership of a realistic Linux incident: establish context, preserve evidence, diagnose a failed Ascend API, make the smallest safe correction, verify recovery, and write the incident record.', 'duration_minutes': 90, 'xp': 100, 'audio_script': 'Welcome to Lesson 2.9: Linux Capstone, Restore the Service.\n\nThis lesson is different. There is no new command list. You already have the pieces: filesystem navigation, text inspection, pipes, permissions, processes, resources, services, logs, SSH, Bash, and Evidence Before Action.\n\nYou are now the engineer on call.\n\nAt 09:17 monitoring reports that Ascend\'s front end is reachable but lesson API requests fail. A deployment completed at 09:12. Someone writes: “Ascend API appears down after deploy. Can someone restart it?”\n\nDo not treat the requested action as the diagnosis.\n\nFirst establish the symptom and scope. The front end loads. API requests fail. The deployment timing is relevant, but correlation is not proof.\n\nConnect to the authorized Linux host with SSH. Before changing anything, verify hostname, whoami, id, and pwd. Confirm the production host and intended administrative identity.\n\nInspect systemctl status ascend-api.service. The service is failed. Record the failure time. Do not restart yet.\n\nRead the journal around the incident: journalctl -u ascend-api.service --since "09:10" --no-pager. Move toward the earliest meaningful failure after deployment rather than the loudest repeated message.\n\nYou find: Failed to load environment file /etc/ascend/app.env: Permission denied.\n\nNow you have a specific failure. Possible explanations include wrong service identity, wrong ownership, wrong permissions, blocked parent-directory traversal, a wrong path in the unit, or another security control.\n\nInspect the unit with systemctl cat ascend-api.service. It runs as user ascend and references /etc/ascend/app.env.\n\nInspect the path and metadata. The parent path permits intended traversal. The file is root:root with mode 600. The ascend service identity cannot read it.\n\nNow use the timeline. Deployment notes show that a script copied the environment file during the 09:12 deployment. The documented intended state is root ownership, ascend service-group ownership, and mode 640: owner read/write, group read, other no access.\n\nThe supported hypothesis is now clear: deployment replaced app.env with metadata that prevented the non-root Ascend service from reading required configuration, so startup failed.\n\nNotice what you did not do. You did not chmod 777. You did not run the application as root. You did not reboot. You did not force-kill a process that was not running.\n\nRestore the documented intended ownership and permission state with the smallest authorized correction. Verify the metadata.\n\nThe service previously failed, so follow the runbook to start or restart the affected unit as appropriate. Then verify across layers.\n\nsystemctl reports active. Fresh logs show the permission error is gone. The backend health endpoint succeeds. A representative lesson API request succeeds. The front end retrieves lesson data.\n\nNow you have evidence of recovery.\n\nBut restoration is not root-cause prevention. Why did the deployment replace the metadata? The deployment procedure copied a protected file without preserving or restoring intended ownership and mode.\n\nThe prevention is not “remember to chmod it.” Improve the mechanism: provision secrets separately, enforce intended state, or add a preflight/post-deploy validation that fails the deployment when ownership or mode is wrong.\n\nDuring the lab you will simulate this incident entirely under your home directory. You will build a timeline, inspect a fictional environment file and logs, reject unsupported shortcuts, make a narrow lab permission correction, and produce an incident report.\n\nYou will also encounter distractions. Disk usage is 72 percent. That is a measurement, not proof. An old warning appears in yesterday\'s logs. Its timing does not match. A teammate suggests kill -9, but there is no running API process to kill.\n\nOperational work is full of plausible distractions. Your job is to determine which evidence explains the symptom.\n\nThink back to Module 0. Evidence Before Action was conceptual. Linux makes it tangible. A service has a process and identity. That identity accesses files. Files have ownership and permissions. Services emit logs. Hosts have finite resources. Remote access crosses a network. Automation changes state.\n\nThink back to Module 1. Git records changes to code and automation. A bad deployment script can be traced, reviewed, corrected, tested, and prevented from recurring.\n\nThese are not isolated topics. They are layers of one delivery system.\n\nLater, AWS adds cloud APIs. Docker adds container boundaries. CI/CD adds automated execution. Terraform adds declarative infrastructure. Kubernetes adds distributed orchestration. But the questions remain:\n\nWhat should the system do?\nWhat is it actually doing?\nWhat evidence proves the difference?\nWhat changed?\nWhat is the smallest safe action?\nHow will I verify recovery?\nHow do we prevent recurrence?\n\nDo not rush the capstone because you have heard the answer. Work the evidence in order. Explain what each command proves. Reject unsafe shortcuts. Build the timeline. Produce the incident record.\n\nWhen you finish, Module 2 is complete.\n\nYou do not need every Linux command memorized. You need a dependable way to think.\n\nEvidence before action. Small changes. Verification after action.\n\nKeep climbing.', 'objectives': ['Apply the complete Module 2 toolkit to a realistic service incident.', 'Build an incident timeline and separate causal evidence from distractions.', 'Diagnose an identity/permission failure from service, log, and filesystem evidence.', 'Choose a least-privilege correction instead of broad workarounds.', 'Verify recovery across systemd, logs, backend health, API behavior, and the user-facing workflow.'], 'content': [{'heading': 'The requested action is not the diagnosis', 'body': 'Begin with the observed symptom and state rather than accepting “restart it” as proof of cause.'}, {'heading': 'Context comes before commands', 'body': 'Confirm host, user, environment, and path before consequential remote work.'}, {'heading': 'Time connects change and failure', 'body': 'Deployment timing makes a change relevant, but logs and state must show how it caused the symptom.'}, {'heading': 'Follow the first meaningful failure', 'body': 'Repeated downstream errors can obscure the initiating event. Build a timeline.'}, {'heading': 'Permissions are identity decisions', 'body': 'Translate ownership and mode into the exact access the service identity receives.'}, {'heading': 'Reject excessive fixes', 'body': 'chmod 777, root execution, rebooting, and force-killing do not address the supported cause.'}, {'heading': 'Recovery requires proof and prevention', 'body': 'Verify multiple layers, document the incident, and correct the deployment mechanism so the same metadata failure is caught or prevented.'}], 'diagram': {'title': 'The requested action is not the diagnosis', 'description': 'Take ownership of a realistic Linux incident: establish context, preserve evidence, diagnose a failed Ascend API, make the smallest safe correction, verify recovery, and write the incident record.', 'nodes': [{'label': 'The requested action is not the diagnosis', 'detail': 'Begin with the observed symptom and state rather than accepting “restart it” as proof of cause.'}, {'label': 'Context comes before commands', 'detail': 'Confirm host, user, environment, and path before consequential remote work.'}, {'label': 'Time connects change and failure', 'detail': 'Deployment timing makes a change relevant, but logs and state must show how it caused the symptom.'}, {'label': 'Follow the first meaningful failure', 'detail': 'Repeated downstream errors can obscure the initiating event. Build a timeline.'}, {'label': 'Permissions are identity decisions', 'detail': 'Translate ownership and mode into the exact access the service identity receives.'}, {'label': 'Reject excessive fixes', 'detail': 'chmod 777, root execution, rebooting, and force-killing do not address the supported cause.'}], 'caption': 'Orient, gather evidence, make the smallest justified change, and verify.'}, 'engineer_perspective': {'title': 'Engineer’s Perspective', 'body': 'Verify multiple layers, document the incident, and correct the deployment mechanism so the same metadata failure is caught or prevented.'}, 'try_it_yourself': {'title': 'Try It Yourself', 'intro': 'Practice in an authorized lab environment and avoid exposing credentials or changing real production services.', 'steps': ['Create ~/ascend-linux-lab/capstone with etc/ascend, logs, and deploy subdirectories.', 'Create a fictional app.env containing no secrets and set its mode to 600.', 'Create timestamped fictional service.log entries for deployment, permission failure, repeated starts, and API failure.', 'Use ls -l, stat if available, head, tail, and grep to gather evidence.', 'Write the hypothetical systemctl status and time-bounded journalctl commands you would use on a real host.', 'Build a five-event incident timeline.'], 'takeaway': 'Explain what each command proves before you use it.'}, 'lab': {'title': 'Lesson 2.9 Lab', 'instructions': ['Create ~/ascend-linux-lab/capstone with etc/ascend, logs, and deploy subdirectories.', 'Create a fictional app.env containing no secrets and set its mode to 600.', 'Create timestamped fictional service.log entries for deployment, permission failure, repeated starts, and API failure.', 'Use ls -l, stat if available, head, tail, and grep to gather evidence.', 'Write the hypothetical systemctl status and time-bounded journalctl commands you would use on a real host.', 'Build a five-event incident timeline.', 'Translate mode 600 and explain why a non-owner service identity cannot read the file.', 'Add the scenario fact that intended state is root:ascend with mode 640 and state the root-cause hypothesis.', 'Reject chmod 777, running as root, reboot, and kill -9 with an evidence-based explanation.', 'On the lab file only, change mode 600 to 640 and verify the exact permission change.', 'Write the real-world recovery action you would take according to the runbook.', 'Create a four-layer verification checklist: systemd, fresh logs, backend/API, front-end workflow.', 'Write an incident report covering impact, timeline, evidence, root cause, correction, verification, and prevention.', 'Propose an automated preflight/post-deploy check that would detect wrong ownership or mode.', 'Finish by explaining how Evidence Before Action changed your response to the incident.']}, 'quiz': [{'question': 'What is the initial capstone symptom?', 'choices': ['Front end loads but API requests fail', 'Disk is definitely full', 'SSH is broken', 'Server requires reboot'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why is the deployment timing relevant but not proof?', 'choices': ['Correlation still needs causal evidence', 'Deployments cannot break services', 'Logs are unnecessary', 'Only CPU proves causes'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Which evidence directly explains startup failure?', 'choices': ['Permission denied reading the required environment file', 'Disk usage is 72%', 'An old warning', 'A restart request'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does mode 600 grant?', 'choices': ['Owner read/write; group and other no access', 'Everyone read/write', 'Owner execute only', 'Group read/write only'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why is chmod 777 a poor fix?', 'choices': ['It grants far broader access than required', 'It makes files invisible', 'It deletes ownership', 'It disables systemd'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does least privilege suggest?', 'choices': ['Grant only required documented access', 'Run everything as root', 'Give everyone write access', 'Remove all permissions'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why is systemctl active insufficient final verification?', 'choices': ['The process can run while users still see failures', 'systemctl never reports processes', 'Active means failed', 'Logs cannot be checked'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Which is the strongest recovery verification?', 'choices': ['Service state, fresh logs, backend/API health, and user-facing behavior', 'Only chmod exit code', 'Only SSH login', 'Only disk percentage'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What caused recurrence in the scenario?', 'choices': ['Deployment replaced the protected file with incorrect metadata', 'SSH encryption', 'Users opened the front end', 'Health check success'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What is the main transferable capstone skill?', 'choices': ['Use evidence, make the smallest justified change, and verify', 'Memorize every command', 'Restart first', 'Use root whenever possible'], 'correct': 0}], 'reflection': 'At the start of Module 2, Linux may have looked like a collection of commands. Describe the mental model you now have for identity, files, processes, resources, services, logs, remote access, and automation—and which evidence you would seek first in the next unfamiliar failure.'}
